     "content": "# Refinement histories and the missing detail-source law v0.1\n\n14 October 2026 · reading edition 0.14\n\nStatus: development — no registry entry. Exact conditional mathematics and scoped NO-GO results; constructor-team checks only. Native selection OPEN; independent human review PENDING; physical promotion 0. This adds a different-history test to RT01, not a correction of its same-sequence theorem.\n\n## 1. Explicit comparison contract\n\nThe common old carrier C0 is the full oriented simplex chain of a filled tetrahedron: dimensions (4,6,4,1), total15, boundary B0, old metric I. Compare its center-star history Cs, total29, and ordinary barycentric history Cb, total149. For each use a graded chain injection S and affine/exterior chain retraction R, with BS=SB0, RB=B0R, RS=I. Set Q=I−SR and declare\n\nH=RᵀR+QᵀQ, A=H⁻¹BᵀH−B.\n\nThis **conditional unit-detail calibration** is an authored premise, not a source-selected native metric. It preserves the complete old norm and generator: SᵀHS=I and AS=SA0, A0=B0ᵀ−B0. On kerR the pairing is Euclidean. These are direct-sum chain coordinates, not qubits or a tensor donor bank. Rates are compared in the same fixed internal time; rescaling rates or changing metrics is outside the contract.\n\n## 2. Star geometry and exact spectrum\n\nSource C0656 supplies a funded 1→4 center-star topology move. Our additional full graded interface fixes center vertex4 and uses four tetrahedra 0124,0134,0234,1234. S is identity on old degrees0–2 and sends 0123 to 0124−0134+0234−1234. R sends center4 to (e0+e1+e2+e3)/4 and extends by exterior powers. This choice is not inferred from NRF-1's inherited-edge inclusion and child charts.\n\nThe star f-vector is (5,10,10,4), with detail dimensions (1,4,6,3) and detail boundary ranks (1,3,3). The detail Laplacian is exactly5I on detail. The full skew characteristic polynomial is\n\nλ(λ²+4)^7(λ²+5)^7.\n\nThus star retains the old harmonic direction and seven coarse frequency2 pairs, and adds seven detail √5 pairs. All statements refer to the declared weighted generator, not the raw unit-metric star generator.\n\n## 3. Frequency capacity versus symmetry\n\nTheorem HC.1 — frequency capacity. Barycentric detail has squared-frequency5 eigenspace dimensions (2,7,8,3), giving ten √5 pairs. Boundary singular-pair multiplicities at √5 are (2,5,3), versus star (1,3,3). Therefore a 29→149 isometric full-generator embedding exists without symmetry constraints; a graded chain isometric embedding also exists. Choose the shared coarse code and compatible singular pairs in detail. Such encodings are nonunique and not source-selected, local or automatically S4-covariant.\n\nTheorem HC.2 — S4 obstruction. Average the 24 signed vertex-permutation actions to project onto the trivial representation. Exact intersections with squared-frequency5 have these graded dimensions:\n\n| History | Degree0 | Degree1 | Degree2 | Degree3 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Center-star | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| Barycentric | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n\nHence star has a trivial-representation √5 skew pair absent from the complete barycentric carrier: its coarse sector has only frequencies0 and2. Any S4-equivariant generator-intertwining isometry must preserve both frequency and the trivial representation, and cannot kill a nonzero vector. Therefore no such full star→barycentric embedding exists under this contract. The obstruction does not forbid non-equivariant encodings, another metric, another rate contract or augmented carriers.\n\n## 4. Coarse agreement is not full history equivalence\n\nTheorem HC.3 — lossy coarse comparison. T_b→s=Ss Rb agrees on all common coarse states, but kills all134 barycentric details. Its reverse T_s→b=Sb Rs kills all14 star details. Their compositions are coarse projectors, not identity. Moreover T_b→sᵀ Hs T_b→s=RbᵀRb, whereas Hb=RbᵀRb+QbᵀQb. Coarse observable pullbacks agree, but full fine-state norms and records are not retained. A full terminal bijection Cs↔Cb is impossible by dimensions29≠149.\n\n## 5. A reversible resource completion\n\nTheorem HC.4 — retained-record transfer. Let Ks=kerRs and Kb=kerRb. The map\n\n(x_b,w_s)=(Sb c+z_b,w_s) ↦ (Ss c+w_s,z_b)\n\nis a norm-preserving bijection Cb⊕Ks→Cs⊕Kb with domain pairing Hb⊕I_Ks and target Hs⊕I_Kb. Both sides have163 directions: input149 plus14 separately prepared target-detail directions; output29 plus134 outgoing-record directions. Norms on both sides are ||c||²+||z_b||²+||w_s||². The inverse decodes c and w_s from the target and retains z_b to reconstruct x_b.\n\nWith blank w_s=0, the outgoing z_b still contains every old detail. Discarding or resetting it is not part of this isometry. These counts are not implementation minima, clocks, entropy estimates or an acquired gate. The transfer preserves information but does not claim to intertwine the original complete star and barycentric detail generators. Reset/preparation and protected export require their own source/resource law.\n\n## 6. Why blank details cannot seed themselves\n\nTheorem HC.5 — reducing-subspace no-seed theorem. Suppose A is H-skew, S is an isometry and AS=SA0. For z orthogonal to imS,\n\n〈Az,Sc〉H=−〈z,ASc〉H=−〈z,SA0c〉H=0.\n\nThus imS and its orthogonal detail complement both remain invariant. Pure coarse initial data remains S exp(sA0)c, with identically zero detail. Increasing dimension, declaring H, or adding a time-dependent skew K(s) with K(s)S=0 cannot populate blank details. This is a universal linear-algebra theorem under its assumptions, not a universal impossibility of open refinement dynamics.\n\nIn orthonormal coarse/detail coordinates, a closed skew coupling must have the form\n\nA_C = [[A0, −C†], [C, Ad]].\n\nThen ż=Ad z+C c. Exact full coarse-generator inheritance requires C=0. Nonzero C may seed detail, but introduces a generator defect and reciprocal coarse feedback; the joint norm stays constant. A source law selecting C is not supplied by this construction.\n\nWith external forcing f, ż=A