+    "content": "# Narrative manuscript v0.15 — intake receipt v0.1\n\nStatus: administrative source intake; additional-workstream review candidate; no registry entry or promotion. Physical promotion: 0.\n\n## Identity and custody\n\nTitle: Unified Dynamics: Distinction, Interaction and Persistence.\nSubtitle: A theoretical account of tetrahedral carriers, information access and finite records.\nAuthor on document: Benjamin Walker Mayes. Cover date: 15 October 2026 UTC. Review candidate: 0.15. Format: 56-page A4 PDF.\n\nThe user supplied the complete PDF for runtime ingestion, artifact capture and standby. The original PDF is preserved byte-for-byte. Its Library identity remains libfile_570972bb364881918dbaa4cbc177cd36. Original SHA-256: 54add793a67d8c5f3c68dfd9180afaba5ba74d65179d6643d8bea449f988e6e7.\n\n## Status and relationship to current sources\n\nThis is a separate theoretical synthesis. Its own source-and-scope statement uses the four-paper M5 release dated 4 October 2026, Papers I–III 1.1.1 and Paper IV 0.1. The document pins an earlier repository baseline 4b9c1f7ba03f76324b792f389517161d2bc47e05 and explicitly limits its reconciliation. It does not replace those papers or the Public Atlas reading edition 0.14. The shared v0.15 label is the manuscript version, not a new Atlas edition.\n\nThe PDF's EXACT/conditional/NO-GO/candidate and numerical findings are imported source statements at their original scope. This intake performs no scientific promotion, full proof audit, novelty assessment, independent review or physical test. The attached v0.39 ledger remains historical; current portal amendments and typed provenance remain separate. The CRL-2 attachment labels itself a proposal; the site's 2026-09-14 extension retains its distinct active topology/source scope.\n\n## Runtime retrieval map\n\n- Sections 1-2; PDF pages 3-8: Representation, distinction, binary labels, event versus observation.\n- Sections 3-5; PDF pages 8-11: Oriented chains, coherent flow, signed symmetry and constitutive freedom.\n- Sections 6; PDF pages 12-15: Gluing contracts, copied versus unique carriers, dynamic memory, conditioning.\n- Sections 7-8; PDF pages 15-18: Prepared assembly, shape comparisons, full-state persistence versus readout access.\n- Sections 9; PDF pages 18-20: Projective receipts, covariance and history information.\n- Sections 10-12; PDF pages 20-24: Prepared apparatus, finite capture, renewal, resources and physical bridge.\n- Sections 13; PDF pages 24-25: Native event-source boundaries and joint-state obligations.\n- Sections A-C; PDF pages 25-27: Source crosswalk, comparison contracts and review provenance.\n- Sections D.1-D.8; PDF pages 27-32: Flow/closure proofs, refined joint error, representation and stacked memory.\n- Sections D.9-D.11; PDF pages 32-40: Conditioning, sampling, metric-scoped transfer and supplied channels.\n- Sections D.12-D.14; PDF pages 40-46: Receipt alphabet, history graph, kernel freedom, winding and affine control.\n- Sections D.15; PDF pages 46-48: Passive hysteresis candidate, finite activity, noise and symmetry collision.\n- Sections D.16; PDF pages 49-51: Sampled guards, event-time intervals, precision/cadence and storage.\n- Sections D.17; PDF pages 51-52: Receipt consistency, correlated error covariance and blind error subspace.\n- Sections E and references; PDF pages 53-56: Reported companion packages, development disclosures and source references.\n\n## High-value source claims retained for later work\n\n- Section 6.5 / D.8: the stated stacked-family theorem concerns independent copied cell dynamics, fresh-vertex face attachments, all three nonzero constant exchange weights, arbitrary copied initial states and an exact autonomous linear realization retaining the normalized visible coordinates. It states n=15t, m=8t+7, rank(Q;QA)=15t and additional state dimension 7(t-1). This is not the native unique-simplex generator or a count of physical memory bits.\n- Section 6.6 / D.9: exact identifiability is distinguished from noise robustness, measurement scale and actual acquisition cost. The PDF reports exact and numerical conditioning certificates; those companion bytes are not attached here.\n- Sections 2.5 and D.15: hysteresis is an explicitly supplied passive response candidate with thresholds and initial memory. Symmetric simultaneous hits obstruct deterministic equivariant selection of a single address without further data.\n- D.16: polling can miss an inclusive guard touch despite positive ideal dwell. Faithful address-wise detection uses additional excursion, persistence, error and cadence premises. Event-time intervals preserve unresolved order; acquisition order is not automatically ideal event order.\n- Section 9.4 / D.17: H=CS is a signless receipt-observation identity. Its residual can vanish under the wrong latent dynamics and under consistent common-field errors. Error cross-covariances and residual mean matter; receipt consistency alone is not an independent physical prediction.\n- Section 13.1: native event/source, transport, reservoir, generator-change and calibrated physical-observable obligations remain open. G05/G06/G09 are source-open in this manuscript's stated scope.\n\n## Verification and boundaries\n\nCompleted: PDF metadata and 56-page count; complete text extraction; cover render inspection; original-byte hash; page-wise text hashes and source retrieval map; byte comparison of captured and public copies. Main argument and the relevant proof/assumption sections were inspected for intake. This is not a layout audit or mathematical certification.\n\nThe complete text is a searchable derivative made by pdftotext -layout; line breaks, bars, radicals, superscripts and other mathematical typography can be lossy. The original PDF controls any disputed formula. Page-index hashes refer to extracted text, not original page objects.\n\nThe manuscript references companion releases and authored replay scripts, including verify_conditioning.py, verify_admission.py, verify_sampled_acquisition.py and verify_research_batch4.py. Their stated PASS counts are reported by the document; those companion packages were not supplied in this turn, inspected or executed as part of intake. No external references were independently revalidated here.\n\nThe unavailable Gap2_FINAL_Finite_Genesis.png was not read or used. Raw notebooks, private correspondence and unrelated files are excluded. No source-law derivation or follow-on research is authorized by ingestion alone. Stand by after capture.\n\n\n## Searchable extracted manuscript\n\n     Unified Dynamics: Distinction, Interaction and Persistence\n   A theoretical account of tetrahedral carriers, information access and finite records\n\n\n                                     Benjamin Walker Mayes\n\n                      15 October 2026 (UTC) — Review candidate 0.15\n\n\nContents\n   Abstract . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .    3\n\n1. The problem: how does change produce things that last?                                             3\n   1.1 Object, representation and observation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .       4\n   1.2 Exact representation and controlled approximation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          4\n   1.3 Scope, contribution and relation to prior work . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         5\n   1.4 Assumptions and mathematical domains . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           5\n\n2. Distinction: specify the alternatives before explaining their evolution                            6\n   2.1 State, observation and resolvability . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .     6\n   2.2 A finite binary label register . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   7\n   2.3 Comparing event labels and composing a history . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           7\n   2.4 A reference kernel exposes what counting cannot select . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         7\n   2.5 When does continuous change count as an event? . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           7\n\n3. Why tetrahedral structure enters                                                                   8\n   3.1 Relationships before metric . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      8\n   3.2 Chain conventions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      8\n\n4. Interaction: what the architecture actually does                                                    9\n   4.1 The declared coherent flow . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        9\n   4.2 A local account of exchange . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .       9\n   4.3 Single-cell recurrence . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   10\n\n5. Symmetry: organize interactions without inventing coeﬀicients                                10\n   5.1 Symbolic strengths and invariant exchange sectors . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11\n   5.2 Quadratic context and its limits . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11\n\n6. Shapes change function; topology changes require another rule                                   12\n   6.1 Glued spectra . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 12\n   6.2 Shared interfaces and ownership . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 12\n   6.3 Equal exterior boundary is insuﬀicient for dynamical equivalence . . . . . . . . . . 12\n   6.4 A transfer contract that retains the interface residual . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 13\n   6.5 A family law for gluing and dynamic memory . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 13\n   6.6 Exact access and reliable readout are different requirements . . . . . . . . . . . . . 14\n\n7. Persistence and formation                                                                    15\n   7.1 State preservation does not imply attraction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 15\n\n\n                                                   1\n\f   7.2 An explicit prepared growth construction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      15\n   7.3 What a preference claim must specify . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      16\n   7.4 What the five-cell calculation adds . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   16\n\n8. A distinction can survive while a readout loses access                                     17\n   8.1 Readout error under a declared noise law . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 18\n\n9. Reconstruction: what must a receipt contain?                                                   18\n   9.1 Why stocks and currents may be insuﬀicient . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 18\n   9.2 A suﬀicient real receipt . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 19\n   9.3 Complex phase receipts . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 19\n   9.4 Prediction and uncertainty . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 19\n   9.5 A readable history functional also needs record capacity . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 20\n\n10. From readable state to finite record apparatus                                             20\n   10.1 Prepared history propagation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 20\n   10.2 Timed protection and static retention differ . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 21\n   10.3 Why permanent capture needs another mechanism . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 22\n\n11. Renewal, preparation and complete resource cost                                             22\n   11.1 Renewal moves correlations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 22\n   11.2 Coherent supply is a resource . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 22\n   11.3 What a complete cost comparison