 Appendix D.7 iden-\ntifies its spectrum with ours. The single-cell spectrum is established background. Weighted\nsimplicial Dirac constructions also predate this manuscript [19]. Norm conservation is a stan-\ndard consequence of skew-adjointness.\nPhase recovery from relative offsets belongs to the established synchronization problem [13].\nThe noiseless support-tree receipt in §9 gives a suﬀicient reconstruction under supplied nonzero-\nsupport data and component references. It is not a new general synchronization algorithm. The\nclock in §10 uses engineered perfect state transfer [14]; prefix dressing adds a supplied gate word,\nnot a new origin for the clock.\nThe main added result is the stacked-family memory theorem in §6.5, supported by a structural\nrank proof. The selected glued spectra, five-cell comparison, two-cell observation obstruction\nand retained interface provide concrete examples. General observability and projection-memory\nprinciples are established background [20,21]. Results inherited from Papers I-IV are cited as\nsuch. The present synthesis explains how their assumptions connect and where they do not.\nWe make no priority claim that these finite examples are absent from all prior literature; a\ncomprehensive novelty assessment remains a submission task.\n\n1.4 Assumptions and mathematical domains\n\n\n\n\n                                                   5\n\fDomain                           Declared premises                Limit on interpretation\nReal carrier                     Finite downward-closed           Coordinate stocks are model\n                                 complex; one coordinate per      quantities\n                                 nonempty simplex; increasing\n                                 orientation; Euclidean\n                                 simplex metric\nFixed flow                       Constant real skew generator;    Full-state conservation does\n                                 no supplied switching during     not imply attraction or\n                                 the interval                     retention\nObservation                      Named map, preparation           A readout is not\n                                 alternatives, known time;        automatically an apparatus\n                                 noise only where stipulated      or record\nComplex receipts                 Nonzero-support forest and       Relative phases require\n                                 one supplied root phase per      references and acquisition\n                                 component\nQuantum apparatus                Separate finite complex          No derivation of quantum\n                                 Hilbert spaces; declared joint   theory from the real carrier\n                                 inputs, Hamiltonians and\n                                 comparator\nResources                        Count supplied donors,           A subsystem energy or\n                                 references, clocks, switching    marginal error is not total\n                                 and exhaustion on the same       cost\n                                 domain\nPhysical bridge                  No calibrated length,            Physical entropy-area\n                                 laboratory time, action scale    normalization remains open\n                                 or geometric metric is\n                                 supplied\n\n\nThe symbol 𝒳 in §2.1 denotes a general state space; the binary register Θ in §2.2 is a separate\nlabel space. Model parameter 𝑠 and apparatus time 𝑡 are not identified. Entropies use natural\nlogarithms and are dimensionless unless a physical conversion is separately declared.\n\n\n2. Distinction: specify the alternatives before explaining their\nevolution\n2.1 State, observation and resolvability\nLet a declared state space be 𝒳 and an observation map be 𝑂 ∶ 𝒳 → 𝒴. Two states 𝑥, 𝑦 are dis-\ntinguishable under this exact observation when 𝑂(𝑥) ≠ 𝑂(𝑦). Under a noisy or finite-resolution\nobservation, distinguishability additionally depends on the output distributions, permitted de-\ncisions and error criterion.\nThis operational definition makes the model’s alternatives explicit. It does not imply that phys-\nical existence depends on observation, nor that every state difference is already an irreversible\nrecord. Distinction under a readout is relative to a specified map.\nFor real chain states, the observation might retain signed coordinates or only their squares.\nFor complex states, it might retain amplitudes, phases or bilinear receipts. These alternatives\npreserve different information. A narrative that calls them all “information” without naming\nthe map would conceal the central problem.\n\n\n\n                                               6\n\f2.2 A finite binary label register\nOn four vertices, the tetrahedral edge graph has three opposite-edge matchings:\n\n\n                       𝑀1 = {01, 23},    𝑀2 = {02, 13},    𝑀3 = {03, 12}.\n\nAssigning one bit to each defines Θ = 𝔽32 . Its eight labels separate into Hamming-weight sectors\nof dimensions 1, 3, 3, 1. Vertex permutations induce an onto action 𝑆4 → 𝑆3 on the matchings,\nwith Klein-four kernel [1, §3].\nThe labeled product set 𝑆4 × Θ has 24 ⋅ 8 = 192 elements. Under the specified reference-edge\nprojection, its six edge fibers each have 32 elements. These are exact counts of declared objects.\nThey do not establish a physical coupling, a 32-stage history or a clock period. On the same\nfiber one can supply an identity update, a transposition or a 32-cycle.\nThe binary register and the amplitude carrier below therefore have different roles. The first\norganizes labels; the second supports continuous state evolution. A source law relating them\nmust be supplied or derived. Counting their coordinates does not provide that law.\n\n2.3 Comparing event labels and composing a history\nA receipt alphabet can distinguish event types without specifying which events can follow each\nother. The additional working notes [24] motivate a conditional 44-label alphabet: eight fixed-\nweight labels, eighteen onset labels and eighteen extinction labels, including address and parity.\nIt has 946 unordered comparisons of distinct labels. Only 400 pairs have even one ordering with\ncompatible incoming and outgoing Hamming weights. Full bit, frame and transport rules may\nreduce this set further. These counts organize possible comparisons; an admissible event graph\nand its update kernel remain separate obligations.\nThe onset/extinction block illustrates why numerical matches need typed provenance. Both\nits same-address subset and its reverse-weight-step subset have 108 pairs, yet their intersection\ncontains only 36. They are different subsets. Neither their count ratio nor an assigned address\ncost determines a winding increment. Appendix D.12 derives the counts and shows that a\nwinding or cost is recoverable from a receipt precisely when it is constant on each receipt fiber.\n\n2.4 A reference kernel exposes what counting cannot select\nAppendix D.13 supplies a declared fine-history model for that alphabet: a permutation of the\nthree pair addresses followed by no flip or one addressed flip. Its eight Boolean vertices admit\n192 action-distinct edges. Separating the identity/no-flip null gives 44 active receipt labels plus\none null symbol. This all-open graph realizes every weight-compatible active receipt word, but\nits admission rule is supplied rather than derived from the continuous carrier.\nA complete normalized kernel can be written on this graph. Uniform primitive controls give\nstationary weight populations (1, 3, 3, 1)/8. Other kernels with the same permutation symmetry\nand positive action support realize every positive stationary weight distribution. Thus a valid\nevent alphabet and its symmetry do not determine formation preferences. The kernel’s source\nand physical clock remain additional obligations; this is a prepared classical model, not the skew\nflow of §4 [25].\n\n2.5 When does continuous change count as an event?\nA changing stock does not name its own event. A response rule must specify which change\nmatters and what persists after it. Appendix D.15 tests one explicit candidate: each opposite-\nedge stock drives a memory bit with distinct on and off thresholds. Once those thresholds\n\n                                                7\n\fand an initial memory are supplied, the carrier trajectory determines a unique sequence of\nsimultaneous update batches. The gap between thresholds gives finite activity and a bound that\nsurvives specified observation error.\nThe construction also reveals a constraint on the narrative. A symmetric vertex preparation\ncan drive all three pair addresses across a threshold together. Choosing exactly one address\nthen requires an additional reference, asymmetric state or stochastic mechanism; the coherent\ntrajectory alone does not distinguish one. The natural covariant batch is outside the reference\ngraph’s one-flip primitive alphabet, although its endpoint belongs to the composition group. This\ncandidate connects a carrier signal to explicit event semantics without deriving that semantics,\na native geometry update or a free physical measurement [27].\nReading an event requires another contract. Finite-rate threshold polling can miss a guard touch\neven when the ideal response has a positive dwell time. Appendix D.16 supplies conservative\nsampled guards and an explicit excursion/persistence condition under which every address-wise\nevent is confirmed within a bounded delay. Cross-address order needs additional timing sepa-\nration or uncertainty receipts: one simultaneous carrier batch can split across several detection\nticks. The distinction matters before a recorded order is used to compute a history-dependent\nquantity [29].\n\n\n3. Why tetrahedral structure enters\n3.1 Relationships before metric\nA state list does not determine which parts interact. A simplicial complex makes those rela-\ntionships explicit through containment and shared boundaries. A filled tetrahedron includes\nvertices, edges, faces and a cell. Its chain dimensions are\n\n\n                           𝑓(Δ3 ) = (4, 6, 4, 1),       dim 𝒞(Δ3 ) = 15.\n\nIt is the smallest three-dimensional simplex. This supplies a compact architecture with a closed\ntwo-dimensional boundary and several incidence levels. It provides a tractable setting for ex-\nchange within a cell and coupling between cells.\nThis explains why it is useful to investigate tetrahedra. It does not prove uniqueness among\ncandidate architectures or derive the dimension of physical space. A topological cell dimension,\na chain-register dimension and an apparatus Hilbert-space dimension are distinct objects.\n\n3.2 Chain conventions\nLet 𝐾 be a finite oriented tetrahedral simplicial complex, downward closed under faces. Each\nnonempty simplex occurs once. Increasing vertex order fixes the basis orientation. Set\n\n                                                   3\n                                     𝒞(𝐾) = ⨁ 𝐶𝑘 (𝐾; ℝ).\n                                               𝑘=0\n\n\nFor an oriented simplex,\n\n                                              𝑘\n                              𝜕𝑘 [𝑣0 … 𝑣𝑘 ] = ∑(−1)𝑗 [𝑣0 … 𝑣𝑗̂ … 𝑣𝑘 ].\n                                             𝑗=0\n\n\n\n\n                                                    8\n\fWrite 𝐵𝑘 for its matrix. Every codimension-two face appears twice with opposite signs, giving\n𝐵𝑘−1 𝐵𝑘 = 0. The simplex basis is orthonormal in the present model. This is a declared\nEuclidean inner product on coordinates, not a derived physical spacetime metric.\nThe filled tetrahedron, its boundary sphere and its edge graph are different carriers. Replac-\ning one by another changes the chain spaces, homology and operators. Their shared visual\nvocabulary cannot make their equations interchangeable.\n\n\n4. Interaction: what the architecture actually does\n4.1 The declared coherent flow\nOn the direct-sum chain space define [1, §4]\n\n                             0           −𝐵1  0   0\n                           ⎛\n                           ⎜𝐵1𝑇           0  −𝐵2  0 ⎞⎟                 𝑑𝑐\n                      𝒜𝐾 = ⎜\n                           ⎜                         ⎟,                   = 𝒜𝐾 𝑐.\n                           ⎜ 0           𝐵2𝑇\n                                              0  −𝐵3 ⎟\n                                                     ⎟                 𝑑𝑠\n                                ⎝ 0       0  𝐵3𝑇  0 ⎠\n\nThe parameter 𝑠 has no laboratory unit assigned. The operator is skew, so\n\n                                 𝑑 𝑇\n                                    (𝑐 𝑐) = 0,       𝑒𝑠𝒜𝐾 ∈ 𝑂(𝒞(𝐾)).\n                                 𝑑𝑠\n\nMore strongly, every pair of solutions preserves its Euclidean separation:\n\n\n                                      ‖𝑐(𝑠) − 𝑐(𝑠)‖\n                                               ̃    = ‖𝑐(0) − 𝑐(0)‖.\n                                                               ̃\n\nThis tells us what the model protects: full-state norm and distance. It does not yet tell us what\na particular readout retains or how disturbances affect the model.\nThe chain identity also gives\n\n\n                         −𝒜2𝐾 = 𝐿𝐾 ,           (𝐿𝐾 )𝑘 = 𝐵𝑘𝑇 𝐵𝑘 + 𝐵𝑘+1 𝐵𝑘+1\n                                                                       𝑇\n                                                                           ,\n\nwith missing endpoint terms zero. Thus the flow’s frequencies follow from the declared combina-\ntorial Laplacian. These are conventional incidence/adjoint constructions [11,12]; the theoretical\nwork lies in their specified carriers and consequences, not in presenting skew norm conservation\nas a new principle.\n\n4.2 A local account of exchange\nFor adjacent simplices 𝜎 ⊂ 𝜏 , define\n\n\n                                  