e ray, with explicit zeros, independently of forest choice. Root signs\nremain necessary because simultaneous sign reversal of a support component leaves its stocks\nand pair products unchanged. The receipt’s suﬀiciency is deterministic identifiability under the\nsupplied data, not an authentication or acquisition theorem.\n\n9.3 Complex phase receipts\nFor complex coeﬀicients define [2, §6]\n\n                                                                𝐵𝜎𝜏 𝒥𝜎𝜏\n                              𝒥𝜎𝜏 = 2𝐵𝜎𝜏 𝑐𝜎 𝑐𝜏 ,        𝑢𝜎𝜏 =           .\n                                                                 |𝒥𝜎𝜏 |\n\nOn nonzero support, 𝑢𝜎𝜏 = 𝑧𝜎 𝑧𝜏 , where 𝑧𝜎 = 𝑐𝜎 /|𝑐𝜎 |. As in noiseless synchronization [13], supply\none root phase per component and propagate forward by 𝑧𝜏 √= 𝑧𝜎 𝑢𝜎𝜏 , using the conjugate ratio\nbackward. Together with normalized stocks this recovers 𝑐/ 𝑆.\nThe oriented cycle products must equal one. Real-part currents alone do not recover these phases:\nconjugation preserves stocks and real parts while generally changing the declared positive ray.\nReferences are part of the receipt’s required resources.\n\n9.4 Prediction and uncertainty\nOn the full-support filled tetrahedron, the incidence graph has 15 nodes and 28 edges. A frozen\ntree uses 14 edges and leaves 14 chords. For log amplitudes, tree observations are endpoint sums.\nAlternating path sums give an exact integer matrix 𝐶 satisfying 𝐻 = 𝐶𝑆 and 𝑧𝐻 = 𝐶𝑧𝑇 [2, §5].\nPrediction covariance follows as Σ𝐻 = 𝐶Σ𝑇 𝐶 𝑇 . The identity does not certify coverage without a\nprobability model, and shared measurement noise requires residual cross-covariances. Algebraic\nconsistency, statistical uncertainty and physical acquisition are separate claims.\nThe explicit split audited in Appendix D.17 sharpens this distinction. The noiseless chord\nresidual vanishes for every latent history because H=CS, including histories generated by a wrong\ndynamics model. For independently acquired noisy channels, its covariance instead includes\nthe complete training/held-out error blocks. Consistent common-field errors remain invisible,\nand zero residual covariance can conceal a constant bias. The residual is a receipt-consistency\ndiagnostic; interpreting it as a prediction test requires separate state or temporal evidence [30].\n\n\n\n                                                   19\n\f9.5 A readable history functional also needs record capacity\nA history receipt can identify endpoints while hiding an accumulated quantity. Appendix D.13\ngives an explicit graph-cycle diagnostic whose repeated active receipt word has 𝑁 + 1 possible\ntotal increments despite known initial and final Boolean states. Exact recovery of that total\nneeds at least 𝑁 + 1 supplemental symbols, or ⌈log2 (𝑁 + 1)⌉ fixed-length bits. Merging nulls\ninto that code increases the requirement to 2𝑁 + 1 symbols. These are conditional worst-case\nbounds for a declared functional and known receipt word and length, not universal physical\nwinding laws.\nThe same appendix proves a complementary boundary: a scalar additive increment with full pair-\npermutation covariance, frame-composition consistency and flip reversal must be a difference\nof a function of Hamming weight. Every closed history then has zero total. The diagnostic\ndeliberately lies outside those assumptions. Nontrivial winding needs a separately justified state\nand transport rule; a finite record apparatus must then acquire the required information and\naccount for its resource cost [25].\n\n\n10. From readable state to finite record apparatus\nReconstruction tells us what information is suﬀicient if supplied. Record formation asks what\ninteraction produces and retains it. Paper IV makes this apparatus question explicit in finite\ncomplex Hilbert spaces [4]. This changes the mathematical description; it is not a derivation of\nquantum theory from real chain amplitudes.\nFor a declared quantum encoding and equal-prior alternatives, optimal readout error can be\nexpressed through trace distance. Every apparatus claim must name that encoding, its joint\nstate and its readout. We use\n\n\n                                      𝐷tr (𝜌, 𝜎) = 12 ‖𝜌 − 𝜎‖1\n\nfor state comparisons, and operator norm for generator mismatch. No physical unit follows from\nthe convention ℏ = 1.\nThe apparatus includes work registers, reference, fuel, production clock, output, capture flag,\nrecords, renewal donor and retained spectators. A complete comparison includes their correla-\ntions. Good marginals cannot certify a good joint state: equal weights on 110, 101, 011 give each\nbit success probability 2/3 and zero probability of all three succeeding.\n\n10.1 Prepared history propagation\nFor a supplied gate word 𝑈1 , … , 𝑈𝑛 , prefix dressing of the clock\n\n                                𝑛−1\n                       𝐻𝐶 = 𝜅 ∑ √(𝑗 + 1)(𝑛 − 𝑗)(|𝑗 + 1⟩⟨𝑗| + |𝑗⟩⟨𝑗 + 1|)\n                                𝑗=0\n\n\nuses the engineered transfer chain of [14]. Explicitly, supply 𝑛 ≥ 1, 𝜅 > 0, work generators\n𝐻𝑊 , 𝐻̄𝑊 , and unitary gates satisfying [𝑈𝑗 , 𝐻̄𝑊 ] = 0. With prefixes 𝑃0 = 𝐼, 𝑃𝑗 = 𝑈𝑗 ⋯ 𝑈1 , let\n\n                                 𝑛\n                          𝒲 = ∑ |𝑗⟩⟨𝑗| ⊗ 𝑃𝑗 ,     𝐻𝑝 = 𝒲(𝐻𝐶 ⊗ 𝐼)𝒲† .\n                                𝑗=0\n\n\n\n\n                                                20\n\fActual and comparison generators are 𝐼𝐶 ⊗ 𝐻𝑊 + 𝐻𝑝 and 𝐼𝐶 ⊗ 𝐻̄𝑊 + 𝐻𝑝 , with ‖𝐻𝑊 − 𝐻̄𝑊 ‖ ≤ 𝑑\non the full admitted domain. Prepare address 0 independently of the work/spectator state. At\nthe endpoint the comparator applies 𝑃𝑛 up to a common phase and the retained free evolution\n      ̄\n𝑒−𝑖𝑡𝐻𝑊 . Its clock norm is 𝜅𝑛. This specifies the comparison contract of [4, Theorem IV.4]. Its\nendpoint probability is sin2𝑛 (𝜅𝑡), transfer occurs at 𝑇 = 𝜋/(2𝜅), and the address returns at\n2𝑇 . The prefixes and preparation are supplied; the gate-dependent address edges do not prove\nlocality on the work registers.\nIf actual and comparison generators differ by at most 𝑑, Duhamel’s identity gives propagator\ndifference at most 𝑡𝑑 and full-joint trace distance at most min{1, 𝑡𝑑}. This includes the entire\ninterval; a successful endpoint does not erase propagation error.\n\n10.2 Timed protection and static retention differ\nTimed capture supplies a terminal exchange, a fresh flag, an off-switch and a factorized guarded\nbackground. Under those premises the output is protected after pulse-off, with its stated free\nevolution retained [4, §5].\nFor timed capture, output protection follows from the factorization 𝐻bg = 𝐻𝑂 + 𝐻rest , with no\nbackground interaction on output 𝑂 or record 𝑀 , after the supplied contacts are off. It holds\nfor correlated actual inputs, including failures. It preserves whatever output was captured; it\ndoes not turn an incorrect capture into a correct one [4, Theorems IV.6-IV.7].\nA separate static post-launch construction provides a finite window [4, Theorem IV.10]. Sup-\nply matched source/output energies, blank flag and records 𝐿, 𝑀 , 𝐸, and terminal production\nprojector 𝑃𝑛 . Define the involution\n\n\n                       𝐽∗ = 𝑃𝑛 ⊗ SWAP𝑆𝑂 ⊗ 𝑋𝐿 𝑋𝑀 𝑋𝐸 + (𝐼 − 𝑃𝑛 ) ⊗ 𝐼.\n\nThe body free generator 𝐻𝑓 satisfies [𝐽∗ , 𝐻𝑓 ] = 0. A fresh independent clock 𝑄 starts at address\n0, has 𝑁 + 1 addresses (𝑁 ≥ 1) and rate 𝜆 > 0. Dress its first edge with 𝐽∗ and later edges\nwith identity: 𝑊0 = 𝐼, 𝑊𝑗 = 𝐽∗ for 𝑗 ≥ 1, 𝒱 = ∑𝑗 |𝑗⟩⟨𝑗| ⊗ 𝑊𝑗 and 𝐻𝑑 = 𝒱(𝐻𝑄 ⊗ 𝐼)𝒱† . Actual\nand comparison generators are 𝐼𝑄 ⊗ (𝐻𝑓 + 𝑉𝑝 ) + 𝐻𝑑 and 𝐼𝑄 ⊗ 𝐻𝑓 + 𝐻𝑑 , with ‖𝑉𝑝 ‖ ≤ ℎ𝑝 . The\ncomplete target is the evolving clock state tensor the freely evolved body 𝐽∗ 𝜌𝐽∗ , including its\nretained spectator. Distance to this target need not mean successful capture if the incoming\nproduction state is nonterminal.\nUnder these premises, the complete joint bound is\n\n\n                                𝜖(𝑡) ≤ min{1, 𝑡ℎ𝑝 + 2| cos(𝜆𝑡)|𝑁 }.\n\nFor 𝜋/(4𝜆) ≤ 𝑡 ≤ 3𝜋/(4𝜆),\n\n\n                              𝜖(𝑡) ≤ min{1, 3𝜋ℎ𝑝 /(4𝜆) + 2 2−𝑁/2 }.\n\nWriting 𝑟 = | cos(𝜆𝑡)|2𝑁 , the comparison reduced-body distance is at most 𝑟. For incoming\nnonterminal probability 𝑝 and fresh records, its NO_CAPTURE probability is 𝑝 + (1 − 𝑝)𝑟.\nThe smaller reduced-body tail cannot replace the full joint term when clock correlations remain\nrelevant. Appendix D.6 gives a tighter joint bound under explicitly stated fresh-record conditions.\nThe comparator transfers exactly halfway through its cycle and undoes the transfer at a full\ncycle. These are pointwise bounds, not continuously monitored survival probabilities.\n\n\n\n                                                21\n\f10.3 Why permanent capture needs another mechanism\nFor finite time-independent Hermitian 𝐻, an invariant subspace is reducing. Its projector com-\nmutes with 𝐻, so population cannot first enter it from its orthogonal complement. Fixed-\nobservable expectations are finite sums of exponentials; if exactly constant on an open interval,\nanalyticity makes them constant for all time [4, Theorem IV.9].\nThis excludes exact permanent first-entry storage under that closed static contract. It permits\nfinite-window approximation, supplied switching and open-system mechanisms with their own\nresources. The theorem explains why protection cannot simply be inferred from successful\ncoherent transfer.\n\n\n11. Renewal, preparation and complete resource cost\n11.1 Renewal moves correlations\nSupply a donor 𝐷 independent of the old active module 𝐴 and its history 𝑌 , with matched\ncarrier and bare energy operators. Then [4, Theorem IV.12]\n\n\n                         SWAP𝐴𝐷 (𝜌𝐴𝑌 ⊗ 𝜎𝐷 )SWAP𝐴𝐷 = 𝜎𝐴 ⊗ 𝜌𝐷𝑌 .\n\nIndependence means the exact product input written above, including the whole history; equal\ndonor marginals are insuﬀicient. For static renewal the active module includes its capture clock.\nThe identity is kinematic; an implemented exchange requires a generator and a comparison\nagainst running contacts [4, §8.1]. The active module is refreshed; all old correlations are\nexported to donor and history. They are not destroyed. Repetition consumes fresh donor and\noutput slots, and failures consume resources too.\nOn matching full joint domains, admitted bank error 𝜖𝐹 and operation comparisons 𝜖𝑗 compose\nunconditionally as\n\n\n                                  𝜖total ≤ min{1, 𝜖𝐹 + ∑ 𝜖𝑗 }.\n                                                        𝑗\n\n\nThis requires uniform operation bounds on the admitted joint states. Isolated-input results do\nnot automatically meet that requirement. The bank error is charged once; diagnostics already\ncovered by it are not charged again as independent errors.\n\n11.2 Coherent supply is a resource\nThe source constructs a uniform reference packet of length 𝐿 = 2𝑞 from a seed and supplied\ncoherent binary fuel [4, Theorem IV.2]. Here Δ > 0, fuel qubit 𝑗 has Hamiltonian Δ2𝑗 |1⟩⟨1|\nand state |+⟩, and the independent reference begins at seed |𝑛0 ⟩ on a suﬀiciently long finite\nladder. The seed energy 𝑛0 Δ is additional to fuel energy. Matched disjoint exchanges transfer\nthe supplied coherence into the reference. Ideal independent fuel has\n\n\n                      ⟨𝐻𝐹 ⟩ = Δ(𝐿 − 1)/2,       Var 𝐻𝐹 = Δ2 (𝐿2 − 1)/12.\n\nThe exchanges conserve the declared sum of bare energies while transferring the supplied coher-\nence. They do not create that coherence from an unspecified reservoir.\nFor a complete stationary input processed covariantly under the declared additive free-energy\ngenerators, the reference remains stationary. With nondegenerate packet energies, its distance\n\n                                               22\n\ffrom the pure uniform packet is at least 1 − 1/𝐿 [4, Theorem IV.3]. Nonstationary supply can\nevade that particular obstruction, but must enter the ledger.\n\n11.3 What a complete cost comparison includes\nCost requires declared energy operators, initial states, interaction and switching conventions,\nclock preparation, write and readout, retention, renewal, and finite exhaustion. Mean energy,\nvariance, signed switching work, peak demands, time and consumed blank capacity are different\nquantities.\nA zero signed mean switching work under one convention does not establish a free record. Ther-\nmal erasure bounds require additional reservoir and initial-state premises; finite-reservoir cor-\nrections are treated in [15]. A fresh-donor SWAP by itself supplies no thermal bath model. Nor\ndoes a matched swap establish an autonomous router. A static machine interval conserves its\ntotal energy; subsystem bare energies can change against the interaction term.\nThese distinctions explain why a theory of records needs the apparatus’s joint state and full re-\nsource account. Without them, information-per-cost comparisons can omit the very mechanism\nproducing the information.\n\n11.4 What a prepared stationary ratio does and does not explain\nA supplied dissipative channel can have a stable population ratio while retaining coherence\nmuch longer than a population readout suggests. Appendix D.11 examines a two-state channel\nmotivated by the partner working notes [23]. Its stationary\n                                                  √ √ state is diag(4/7, 3/7), but its sharp\ntrace-distance contraction per application is (1 + 2)/ 6 ≃ 0.985599, rather than the proposed\n5/7. Population polarization instead relaxes at magnitude 1/6. The whole state must accompany\na retained-distinguishability claim.\nThe ratio 4/3 is reverse-rate divided by forward-rate for the chosen rates 2/3 and 1/2. An\nexplicit third-state branch changes it to (4/3)𝛼. This is a conditional formation mechanism, not a\nderivation of a coframe ratio from incidence geometry. A channel can be dilated using a supplied\nenvironment, but its mathematical operators specify neither the environment’s Hamiltonian nor\nrenewal and switching costs. Those resources remain required before making an information-\nper-cost comparison.\n\n\n12. Entropy, area and the physical bridge\nEntropy must follow from a declared state and coarse-graining. In an illustrative symmetric\nbinary retention channel, a uniform source bit flipped with probability 𝑝 leaves a uniform memory\nmarginal. Its marginal entropy stays ln 2, while retained source information is\n\n\n                  𝐼(𝑍 ∶ 𝑀 ) = ln 2 − ℎ(𝑝),   ℎ(𝑝) = −𝑝 ln 𝑝 − (1 − 𝑝) ln(1 − 𝑝).\n\nThis elementary example is an additional operational illustration, not a thermodynamic entropy-\nproduction theorem. It shows why memory entropy alone is an inadequate record-quality mea-\nsure.\nA boundary-face count is a combinatorial quantity. Physical boundary area requires a geometric\nmap and units. To derive retained information per area, a capacity or scaling mechanism must\nlink the declared records to that boundary under complete resource constraints. None of the\npreceding conservation or reconstruction results supplies this automatically.\n\n\n\n                                               23\n\fLikewise, the four-paper baseline does not derive a universal gravitational entropy density, New-\nton’s constant or Einstein’s equations. The narrative identifies what such a bridge would have to\nconnect: finite state and records, controlled coarse-graining, physical geometry, thermodynamic\nquantities and independently normalized couplings. Supplying the target normalization would\nnot constitute deriving it. Jacobson’s local-horizon construction [16] assumes an entropy-area\nrelation and a Clausius condition; citing it does not derive those inputs from the UD carrier. No\nBochner-flat Kähler reduction is used or established here.\nCounting events can order histories and track resources. It does not calibrate proper time. A\nmetric, an action scale and a clock map remain additional physical obligations. This manuscript\nmakes no claim that all these bridges are already closed.\n\n\n13. What the theory currently explains, and what it must explain\nnext\nThe finite account already gives a coherent explanatory sequence. Incidence specifies possible\nexchange. A generator calculates its consequences. Symmetry organizes channels while exposing\nfree choices. A restricted readout can conceal a preserved distinction. Suﬀicient receipts restore\nidentifiability when acquired. Prepared capture and renewal specify finite record mechanisms\nand their limits.\nFormation remains partly supplied: boundary attachment is an explicit grammar, but the source\nmust still determine which attachment occurs and how its state and resources transfer. A\nstability or occurrence preference needs the same preparation and disturbance conditions across\ncandidates.\nThe next theoretical targets therefore have a dependency order:\n  1. Specify a native configuration-changing rule and complete state transfer.\n  2. Connect compatibility to formation or survival through that rule.\n  3. Derive a complete production-to-capture trigger and finite router on one joint domain.\n  4. Include reference and clock preparation, failure histories and exhaustion.\n  5. Establish calibrated observable and coarse-graining maps before physical identification.\nEach step answers a question opened by the previous one. That is the manuscript’s organiz-\ning logic: every equation earns its place by explaining a mechanism or delimiting what the\nmechanism can do.\n\n13.1 Reconciled native event-source boundary\nLater source audits qualify the positive interfaces and their limitations [6–8].\nLater sources already provide\n\n\n                                         Θ+ = 𝑃𝑔 Θ− ⊕ 𝜉\n\nand an exact closure/hard-mask pushforward when the receipt xi and relevant local transport\ngates are supplied. The package rechecks 24,576 fixed-gate cases. This closes a conditional map,\nnot the source of xi, gate updates or event occurrence.\nAt identical native pre-state, formal receipt completions xi=000 and xi=111 give different end-\npoints. Both identity and complementation commute with pair permutations. Hence symmetry\nand the fixed pre-event chain trajectory do not uniquely select the endpoint under that interface.\n\n\n\n                                                24\n\fThe positive repair must make the set of admitted next outputs a singleton, or specify a com-\nplete normalized stochastic kernel with receipt availability, null windows and timing separately\ntyped.\nThe balance observable 𝐸3 − 𝐸2 supplies a pre-event signal; treating its crossing as a subdivision\ncommand is a candidate constitutive rule. The historical action/Metropolis acceptance likewise\nfails to select proposal frequency or admissible moves. The later polarity audit shows that uni-\nversal signed-flux/index agreement and simultaneous nontrivial burden selection under binding\nsaturation fail on stated counterexamples.\nThe additional receipt audit sharpens this requirement. A collection of labels with zero individual\nHamming change is not itself a composable history, and the zero change does not determine an\nunobserved winding. To assign a winding-dependent cost, specify the fine-history domain, its\nadmissible composition, the winding functional and what the receipt retains. Appendix D.12\ngives a counterexample to inferring winding from fixed-weight labels alone. This is a source-law\ngap, not an argument against possible laws that supply the missing information.\nThe declared model in Appendix D.13 now closes a narrower development contract: it provides\na complete reference kernel, a scalar no-loop theorem and sharp supplemental-receipt require-\nments for a specified graph-cycle diagnostic. It also proves that symmetry permits any positive\nstationary weight law in the kernel family. None of these statements selects native event prob-\nabilities, a physical winding or its acquisition mechanism. A positive next step is to source the\nadditional orientation, phase, context or reservoir state and its transported increment, then test\nthe enriched receipt fibers.\nTherefore G05/G06/G09 remain source-open. The n