g windows, generator uncertainty and a complete resource ledger.\nThe derivation closes a mathematical conditioning gap while leaving those physical inputs ex-\nplicit.\n\nD.10 Recurrence and transfer under explicit metric contracts\nD.10.1 Incommensurability permits uniformly close returns\nLet 𝐴 be a finite real skew matrix with 𝑟 ≥ 1 distinct positive frequencies 𝜔1 , … , 𝜔𝑟 . For every\ninteger 𝑁 ≥ 1 there is 1 ≤ 𝑞 ≤ 𝑁 𝑟−1 such that, at 𝑠 = 2𝜋𝑞/𝜔1 ,\n\n                                                       2𝜋\n                                        ‖𝑒𝑠𝐴 − 𝐼‖2 ≤      .\n                                                       𝑁\n\nFor 𝑟 > 1, partition the (𝑟 − 1)-torus into 𝑁 𝑟−1 cubes of side 1/𝑁 . Among the 𝑁 𝑟−1 + 1\npoints with coordinates {𝑗𝜔𝑘 /𝜔1 }, 𝑗 = 0, … , 𝑁 𝑟−1 and 𝑘 = 2, … , 𝑟, two share a cube. Their\nindex difference is the required 𝑞, with each phase within 2𝜋/𝑁 of an integer multiple of 2𝜋.\nOrthogonal block diagonalization of 𝐴 reduces the operator error to the largest rotation-block\nerror, bounded by the phase error. Zero modes contribute zero error. For 𝑟 = 1, choose 𝑞 = 1\nand the return is exact. If 𝑟 = 0, the flow is the identity.\nConsequently, irrational frequency ratios exclude an exact common period but do not exclude\napproximate recurrence. If there is no exact period, the approximating times have an unbounded\nsubsequence; otherwise a bounded subsequence would yield an exact return as 𝑁 → ∞. In the\nperiodic case, arbitrarily late exact returns exist. This is a finite-flow statement; no thermody-\nnamic limit, mixing, dissipation or record formation follows from it.\n\nD.10.2 A protected sector for a specified inclusion\nUse the complexes 𝐾𝐴 , 𝐾𝐵 of §6.1 and the coordinate inclusion 𝑇 ∶ ℝ23 → ℝ27 that fixes every\nsimplex of 𝐾𝐴 . This is an inclusion of subcomplexes, not a boundary-fixing 2-to-3 replacement.\nWrite 𝐴 = 𝒜𝐾𝐴 , 𝐵 = 𝒜𝐾𝐵 and 𝐿 = −𝐴2 . With unit metrics,\n\n\n\n\n                                                37\n\f       