receipts of uncertain event times\nD.16.1 A dwell bound does not guarantee threshold detection\nKeep D.15’s passive fixed-carrier model. Its per-address dwell bound concerns the ideal response\nmemory after a guard hit. It does not require the stock to remain above the upper threshold\nuntil a sampler reads it. For example, on the canonical vertex trajectory 𝑞𝑝 = 41 sin2 (2𝑠) choose\nℎ = 1/4 and ℓ = 1/8. The ideal inclusive guard turns on at 𝜋/4 and off at 3𝜋/8. For every odd\ninteger 𝑁 , the grid 𝑠𝑘 = 𝑘𝜋/(2𝑁 ) misses every maximum: equality would require 2𝑘 = (2𝑗+1)𝑁 ,\nimpossible by parity. Direct threshold polling therefore remains off even as the grid becomes\narbitrarily fine.\nThis is a limitation of the threshold-polling observer, not an impossibility theorem for model-\nassisted reconstruction from richer data. Transverse high excursions can also be arbitrarily\nnarrow near grazing; a uniform completeness guarantee needs a quantitative excursion condition\nrather than a cadence alone. The finite-activity theorem in D.15 remains valid.\n\nD.16.2 Certified guards and suﬀicient conditions for faithful acquisition\nLet actual sampling times have maximum gap Δ > 0 and let |𝑦𝑝,𝑘 − 𝑞𝑝 (𝑠𝑘 )| ≤ 𝜀. Initialize the\nsampled response equal to the ideal one. Use the conservative guards\n\n\n                       0→1     if 𝑦𝑝,𝑘 − 𝜀 ≥ ℎ,        1→0   if 𝑦𝑝,𝑘 + 𝜀 ≤ ℓ;\n\notherwise retain the sampled bit. Update all enabled bits in a detection batch, leaving the\ncarrier unchanged. A confirmation implies that the true stock is on the appropriate side of\nits ideal threshold. Large error can prevent confirmation altogether. Unlike D.15’s raw noisy\nthreshold rule, these interval guards need no half-gap condition merely to exclude a wrong-side\nconfirmation; completeness has the additional conditions below [29].\nFor each ideal rising event at 𝜏 , assume the true stock reaches ℎ + 2𝜀 at some 𝑢 ≤ 𝜏 + 2𝜀/𝜈, and\nstays at least that high on [𝑢, 𝑢 + Δ]. A suﬀicient reach condition is 𝑞𝑝′ ≥ 𝜈 > 0 until reaching\nthat level. For a falling event use ℓ − 2𝜀 and −𝑞𝑝′ ≥ 𝜈. These are supplied event-wise excursion\nand persistence hypotheses; they are not consequences of skewness alone.\nThen the matched sampled event is confirmed with delay\n\n\n                             0 ≤ 𝑠det − 𝜏 ≤ 𝐷,         𝐷 = 2𝜀/𝜈 + Δ.\n\nProof. A sample occurs in [𝑢, 𝑢 + Δ]. For a rise, its value satisfies 𝑦 ≥ 𝑞 − 𝜀 ≥ ℎ + 𝜀, so the\nrising guard fires if it has not already done so. A matched confirmation cannot precede the ideal\nevent, because its true stock must already have reached the ideal threshold. The falling proof is\nthe same with inequalities reversed.\nIf these hypotheses hold for every event and 𝐷 < 𝑑∗ , where 𝑑∗ is D.15’s ideal per-address dwell\nbound, induction gives exactly the same toggle word at each address: every event is confirmed\nbefore the next opposite event, and no extra matched event can occur between ideal switches.\nThis claim preserves address-wise words, not necessarily the global order or simultaneity of\ndifferent addresses. For a finite acquisition window the guarantee covers events at least 𝐷 before\nits end; later events require a tail acquisition or an unresolved end-window receipt.\n\nD.16.3 Time intervals preserve what detection order cannot\nWith exact detection time 𝑠𝑖 , retain an event-time interval 𝐼𝑖 = [𝑠𝑖 − 𝐷𝑖 , 𝑠𝑖 ]. With timestamp\nerror at most 𝑗, use\n\n                                                  49\n\f                                     𝐼𝑖 = [𝑠𝑖̂ − 𝐷𝑖 − 𝑗, 𝑠𝑖̂ + 𝑗].\n\nDeclare event 𝑖 definitely earlier than 𝑘 only if sup 𝐼𝑖 < inf 𝐼𝑘 . Overlapping intervals do not\ncertify order or simultaneity. A suﬀicient global ordering condition for ideal times 𝜏𝑖 < 𝜏𝑘 is\n𝜏𝑘 − 𝜏𝑖 > 𝐷𝑖 + 2𝑗, since delays are one-sided. D.15 supplies no uniform gap between different\naddresses, so no positive fixed cadence guarantees global ordering on that full domain. The\nbound Δ refers to actual gaps; if nominal periodic samples have time jitter 𝑗𝑠 , use at most\nΔnominal + 2𝑗𝑠 instead.\nEven an ideal simultaneous batch may split into several detection batches under address-wise\nerror. Such a log records acquisition order. It must not be used as a fine carrier-event order\nwithout further evidence. If 𝑚! different serial orders remain possible and the desired functional\ndistinguishes all of them, recovering a realized order requires at least ⌈log2 (𝑚!)⌉ additional fixed-\nlength bits, conditional on the known event set. This is a necessary storage condition, not a\nmechanism for observing the order; guessing those bits adds no evidence. True batches and\nunresolved order remain valid receipt objects.\n\nD.16.4 Quantization, an exact fixture and a finite storage ledger\nA 𝑏-bit uniform quantizer on [0, 𝑅2 ] with bin-midpoint outputs has maximum quantization error\n𝑅2 /2𝑏+1 . Clipping an analog reading to that interval cannot increase its error relative to a true\nstock in the interval. With analog error at most 𝜀𝑎 , use\n\n\n                                         𝜀 = 𝜀𝑎 + 𝑅2 /2𝑏+1 .\n\nA suﬀicient design inequality for the delay certificate is\n\n\n                              2𝜀𝑎 + 𝑅2 /2𝑏 < 𝜈(𝑑∗ − Δ),          Δ < 𝑑∗ .\n\nThe excursion hypotheses must still be checked. These are logical precision and cadence budgets,\nnot an ADC implementation or a physical energy law.\nFor the canonical unit vertex trajectory, use 𝑅 = 1, ℓ = 1/16, ℎ = 3/16, 𝑏 = 6, 𝜀𝑎 = 0\nand\n√ Δ = 1/64. Then 𝜀 = 1/128. On the required rising/falling bands, the speed is at least\n  39/16 > 3/8; choose 𝜈 = 3/8. The confirmation core has length at least 3/64 > Δ: its stock\nmargin to the peak or trough is 3/64 and |𝑞𝑝′ | ≤ 2. The sinusoidal upper and lower cores are\neach a single interval. Thus\n\n                                   2(1/128)   1    11     1\n                              𝐷=            +    =     <    ≤ 𝑑∗ .\n                                     3/8      64   192   16\n\nSix bits are the minimum for the chosen conservative test 𝐷 < 1/16; five give 19/192 > 1/16.\nThis uses the linear dwell certificate rather than optimizing the angular one, and is not a globally\noptimal digitizer lower bound.\nOn 𝑠𝑛 = 𝑛/64, 0 ≤ 𝑛 ≤ 128, the deterministic midpoint-quantized observer records masks 111 at\nticks 34 and 84. Exact rational sine enclosures certify every bin and guard. Allowing arbitrary\nstock errors within 1/128 gives per-address onset ticks 34–36 and extinction ticks 84–87, with all\ntwelve combinations permitted by the replay envelope. All are within the proved delay bound.\nThe 123 = 1728 joint combinations can produce up to six detection batches from the two ideal\nbatches; no probability or independence law is assumed.\n\n\n                                                  50\n\fThere are 129 scans and 387 scalar stock values. The declared current buffers use 18 stock\nbits, three response bits, an eight-bit tick counter and a three-bit mask buffer. Retaining every\nquantized stock value separately uses 2322 bits. A dense response-word format uses 3(129) = 387\nbits including the initial response. A sparse format with a three-bit initial response, an eight-bit\ncount, and an eight-bit tick plus three-bit mask per record uses 33 bits for the deterministic\ntwo-record trace, or at most 77 for the six-record error envelope. These formats encode the\nobserved response log; they do not replace the raw stock archive when that is required. Interval\ninterpretation, scale, thresholds, quantizer, clock and error contracts require separately supplied\nmetadata.\nStorage capacity is not automatically information gained; a completely known deterministic\npreparation may make its trace predictable. Nor is the log the whole apparatus: sensor prepara-\ntion, back-action, calibration, reference/clock supply, reliable retention, coding/processing and\nrenewal remain uncosted physical resources. This package closes a conditional finite sampling\nand receipt contract. Native event selection, physical implementation, complete energy cost and\nentropy per boundary area remain open.\n\nD.17 Receipt consistency, covariance and errors it cannot reveal\nD.17.1 The declared signless tree map\nThe filled tetrahedron has a Hasse graph with 15 simplex vertices and 28 adjacent-degree con-\ntainment edges. Choose the 14-edge spanning tree and 14 chords specified in the certificate [30].\nFor a declared latent field 𝑧 ∈ ℝ15 , let 𝑆, 𝐻 ∈ {0, 1}14×15 map 𝑧 to endpoint sums on tree and\nchord edges. These are signless observation matrices, not the signed boundary operator or skew\ngenerator.\nThe graph is bipartite by simplex-degree parity. Let 𝑣 have value −1 on even degrees and +1\non odd degrees. Then 𝑆𝑣 = 𝐻𝑣 = 0. Connectivity gives ker 𝑆 = span{𝑣}, so every chord row\nis in the tree row space. Along its odd-length tree path, alternating the endpoint sums cancels\ninternal vertices and retains the two endpoints. Hence\n\n\n                                  𝐻 = 𝐶𝑆,        𝐶 ∈ {−1, 0, 1}14×14 .\n\nFor the supplied tree, rank 𝑆 = 14 and rank 𝐻 = rank 𝐶 = 10. The longest chord path has\nseven edges. A different tree can change 𝐶 and its error amplification. The graph argument is\nstandard incidence/path algebra; the certificate identifies this particular split, not a new general\nreconstruction method.\nA possible magnitude-receipt application requires a separate map. On full nonzero support,\nif a product receipt is |𝐽𝑖𝑗 | = 2|𝐵𝑖𝑗 ||𝑐𝑖 ||𝑐𝑗 |, define 𝑧𝑖 = log |𝑐𝑖 | and 𝑦𝑖𝑗 = log(|𝐽𝑖𝑗 |/(2|𝐵𝑖𝑗 |)).\nThen 𝑦𝑖𝑗 = 𝑧𝑖 + 𝑧𝑗 . Zero amplitudes, phase/sign information and calibration require additional\ntreatment. Applying these signless matrices directly to the chain amplitudes instead defines\nanother observable. The null direction 𝑣 is a measurement gauge: 𝐴𝑣 ≠ 0 for the native\ncanonical generator, so it is not a harmonic carrier mode.\n\nD.17.2 What zero residual covariance establishes\nFor noiseless observations 𝑌𝑆 = 𝑆𝑧 and 𝑌𝐻 = 𝐻𝑧,\n\n\n                                          𝑟 = 𝑌𝐻 − 𝐶𝑌𝑆 = 0\n\n\n\n\n                                                   51\n\fholds for every realization, independently of Gaussianity or the dynamics that generated 𝑧.\nWith finite second moments, Σ𝐻𝐻 = 𝐶Σ𝑆𝑆 𝐶 𝑇 and Σ𝐻𝑆 = 𝐶Σ𝑆𝑆 . If 𝑀 = (𝐼; 𝐶), any positive\nsemidefinite supplied training covariance produces\n\n\n                         Σ28 = 𝑀 Σ𝑆𝑆 𝑀 𝑇 ⪰ 0,        rank Σ28 = rank Σ𝑆𝑆 .\n\nThe equality of ranks follows because 𝑀 has the left inverse selecting its first 14 rows. The 14\nindependent columns of (−𝐶 𝑇 ; 𝐼) lie in the covariance kernel. They span the entire kernel only\nwhen Σ𝑆𝑆 is positive definite; otherwise the kernel is larger.\nThus zero residual from the same generated latent field confirms the enforced observation identity.\nIt does not independently validate the carrier evolution, a source law or a physical prediction.\nAny alternative latent history passed through the same matrices satisfies it too. A covariance\npropagation identity is not an empirical held-out success.\n\nD.17.3 A residual error contract and its blind subspace\nNow declare independently acquired channels with errors, allowing arbitrary correlations:\n\n                     ̃𝑆 = 𝑆𝑧 + 𝑒𝑆 ,\n                     𝑌                   ̃𝐻 = 𝐻𝑧 + 𝑒𝐻 ,\n                                         𝑌                     𝑟 = 𝑒𝐻 − 𝐶𝑒𝑆 .\n\nWriting 𝑅𝑖𝑗 = Cov(𝑒𝑖 , 𝑒𝑗 ), finite second moments give\n\n\n                         Cov(𝑟) = 𝑅𝐻𝐻 + 𝐶𝑅𝑆𝑆 𝐶 𝑇 − 𝑅𝐻𝑆 𝐶 𝑇 − 𝐶𝑅𝑆𝐻 .\n\nThe cancellation of 𝑧 is pointwise, so this formula does not require the errors to be independent\nof 𝑧. For zero-mean independent equal-variance errors across all 28 channels it becomes 𝜎2 (𝐼 +\n𝐶𝐶 𝑇 ), positive definite when 𝜎 > 0. For componentwise error bounds,\n\n\n                                         ‖𝑟‖∞ ≤ 𝜀𝐻 + 7𝜀𝑆\n\non this tree. The row-specific coeﬀicient is its path length, ranging from three to seven here.\nThese are supplied noise contracts, not a derived sensor law. The residual mean must also